  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1224 PM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nMonday...Dry and warmer as a temporary upper ridge over the region \ngradually pushes east, with the ridge axis pushing east through the \nafternoon as the next feature approaches from the west. Plan on high \ntemps in the 50s for the high valleys, and 50s to around 60F for the \nplains. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday...Models are still in agreement on another \nquick-moving system crossing the region mid-week as an open wave, \nbringing another shot of snow to the higher terrain late Tuesday \nthrough Wednesday. Wind-driven snow will occur along the Continental \nDivide, with advisory snow amounts likely for the eastern San Juan \nMts. Across the eastern plains, strong southwest flow and lee \ntroughing will help boost temps well into the 60s. High temps across \nthe plains both days will be in the upper 50s to mid 60s, while the \nhigh valleys will warm into the upper 40s to mid 50s on Tue, then \njust the 40s on Wed.\n\nThursday Through Saturday...A dry cold front dropping south across \nthe eastern plains late Wed night will drop temps a few degrees \nacross the plains on Thu, otherwise conditions remain dry until late \nThu night when the next system starts to move into western CO. \nBeyond that, forecast confidence drops off. There remains a lot of \nquestions concerning the next weather feature for the region \nbeginning Friday, with long range models in disagreement on how this \nwill pan out. Models do indicate another system pushing across the \nregion late Thu night through Sat, but whether this will be another \nquick hit or something more substantial remains a mystery. Key \nphrase for now will be unsettled. Moore",
